.TH LIBESDM_EGD_CLIENT 3 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_egd_client \- client library for the ESDM EGD interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_egd_client.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_egd_client
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm_egd_client
speaks the EGD (Entropy Gathering Daemon) protocol to
.BR esdm\-server (8),
which serves it when started with
.B \-\-egd_socket
or through the shipped EGD socket units.
.PP
The reason to use this rather than
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
is reach: the EGD interface needs nothing but a single UNIX domain stream
socket \- no protobuf, no shared memory, no second socket \- so it works in
places the RPC interface cannot go, such as a chroot that can be handed
exactly one socket. The price is the protocol: it is a legacy one that can
neither express prediction resistance nor transfer more than
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER
(255) bytes per request, though this client splits larger requests
transparently.
.SS Clients and concurrency
A client owns exactly one connection. The EGD protocol carries no request
identifiers \- responses are matched to requests purely by their order on the
stream \- so all operations of one client are serialized against each other and
one request is in flight at a time. An application that wants concurrency uses
several clients.
.PP
That serialization survives a caller that does not: the lock behind it is a
robust one, so a thread killed in the middle of an operation hands the client
to the next caller instead of wedging it. The connection it left at an unknown
offset of the command stream is dropped.
.SS Reconnecting
All operations are thread safe and reconnect on their own:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A client whose initial connect failed \- because the ESDM is not up yet \- is
still handed back usable and connects on first use. A consumer initialized
before the daemon exists, such as an OpenSSL provider loaded during boot, keeps
working once the daemon arrives.
.IP \(bu 2
A transfer that fails halfway leaves the peer's parser at an unknown offset, so
the connection is dropped rather than reused and the operation is retried once
on a fresh one. A daemon restart between two requests is therefore invisible to
the caller.
.IP \(bu 2
A descriptor inherited across
.BR fork (2)
refers to the parent's connection, and two processes interleaving commands on
it would desynchronize the stream. A fork handler closes every inherited
connection in the child; the next operation there opens one of its own. Should
the fork have bypassed the handler \- a direct
.BR clone (2),
say \- the client notices at its next use and reconnects then.
.RE
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_alloc(struct esdm_egd_client **" client ", const char *" socket_path ", unsigned int " timeout_ms ");"
Allocate a client and connect it. Release it with
.BR esdm_egd_client_free ().
.IP
.I socket_path
selects the EGD socket; NULL or the empty string takes the path from the
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
environment variable, and failing that the socket path this ESDM was built
with.
.IP
.I timeout_ms
bounds how long the client waits for the peer in one step \- the connection
setup, or one transfer of a request or its response \- before treating it as
failed. The socket is non\-blocking and every wait is a poll of its own, so this
covers the connect as well, which a receive timeout cannot. 0 selects a default
generous enough for the blocking read command to wait for an ESDM that is not
operational yet; unreasonably large values are capped.
.IP
Returns 0 on success, and a negative value only when no client could be
allocated. A failure to connect is not an error here \- it is reported through
the ESDM logger and the client is usable regardless.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_egd_client_free(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ");"
Close the connection and release the client. Passing NULL is a no\-op.
.TP
.BI "const char *esdm_egd_client_socket_path(const struct esdm_egd_client *" client ");"
The socket path this client talks to. The string is owned by the client.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Obtain
.I buflen
bytes of random data, waiting for the ESDM to become operational. Requests
larger than one protocol transfer are split transparently. On failure
.I buf
is cleansed, so a partial transfer can never be mistaken for a complete one.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random_nonblock(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", size_t *" generated ");"
Obtain random data without waiting. The ESDM answers with as much as it can
deliver right away, which is nothing at all while it is not operational yet;
the amount actually delivered is stored in
.IR generated .
.I buflen
is at most
.BR ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER .
Use this only where a short answer is acceptable \- a seed consumer wants
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_entropy_count(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint32_t *" entropy_bits ");"
Entropy the ESDM currently holds, in bits.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_write_entropy(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", const uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", uint32_t " entropy_bits ");"
Insert at most
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER
bytes into the auxiliary pool of the ESDM. The protocol allows attaching an
entropy claim to the data, but the ESDM only honors it for a caller running as
UID 0 and inserts everybody else's data without any entropy credit. An
unprivileged caller passing a non\-zero
.I entropy_bits
is therefore not an error \- the claim is simply dropped.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_pid(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", pid_t *" pid ");"
PID of the daemon serving the socket.
.SH RETURN VALUE
All functions returning
.B int
return 0 on success and a negative errno value on error.
.SH SYSCALL DISCIPLINE
Once connected, the operations stay within
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR poll (2),
.BR close (2),
.BR getpid (2),
.BR clock_gettime (2),
.BR nanosleep (2)
and
.BR getsockopt (2).
That is deliberate: it is the set a seccomp confined process is typically still
allowed, OpenSSH's pre\-auth sandbox being the reference case. In particular
.BR send (2)/ recv (2)
are avoided \- they are
.BR sendto (2)/ recvfrom (2),
which that filter answers with
.B SECCOMP_RET_KILL
\- and
.B MSG_NOSIGNAL
is therefore not available, so a write is preceded by a poll that catches a
vanished peer before it could raise
.BR SIGPIPE .
.PP
Establishing a connection is a different matter: it needs
.BR socket (2)
and
.BR connect (2),
which such a sandbox does not permit and does not merely fail but kills the
process for. A client that has to serve a sandboxed process must therefore be
connected before the sandbox is entered \- which is why
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ()
connects rather than deferring it. A process that forks into a sandbox cannot
be served at all, since the fork forces a reconnect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
Path of the EGD socket, used when
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ()
is called without an explicit one.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm_egd_client.h>

int get_random(uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen)
{
	struct esdm_egd_client *client;
	int ret;

	ret = esdm_egd_client_alloc(&client, NULL, 0);
	if (ret)
		return ret;

	ret = esdm_egd_client_get_random(client, buf, buflen);

	esdm_egd_client_free(client);

	return ret;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR libesdm_aux_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
